    "_id": "post_308",
    "post_type": "discussion",
    "author_id": "usr_34",
    "title": "Continuous batching in LLM serving: implementing vLLM PagedAttention in a custom Rust runtime",
    "content": "Traditional naive batching in transformer model inference wastes massive amounts of GPU memory on padding tokens because request lengths vary wildly.\n\nPagedAttention treats Key-Value (KV) cache tensors like virtual memory pages in an operating system. KV blocks are allocated on demand across non-contiguous VRAM rather than pre-allocating static buffers for worst-case token lengths.\n\nThis allows dynamic memory sharing between parallel decoding sequences and eliminates internal memory fragmentation almost completely.",
