    "_id": "post_319",
    "post_type": "discussion",
    "author_id": "usr_23",
    "title": "Measuring write-ahead log fsync latency jitter: ext4 with direct I/O vs XFS on enterprise NVMe",
    "content": "When a transactional database commits a transaction, the write-ahead log (WAL) buffer must be flushed to non-volatile storage using `fdatasync()`.\n\nDuring heavy checkpointing, ext4 log journaling can introduce noticeable tail latency spikes due to JBD2 lock contention. By contrast, XFS's decoupled metadata logging architecture provides much more predictable tail latency for write-heavy database workloads.\n\nTesting both filesystems under realistic write workloads convinced us to format our dedicated database data volumes with XFS.",
